  "summary": "I have graduated from Laney College for my A.A degree in Mathematics and from San Jose State University for my B.S degree in Computer Science. While studying at school, I have worked as a tutor in Math for all lower division Math levels and a tutor in Computer Science for most the undergraduate courses. \n\nI have earned a Tutoring Certificate when working as a Computer Science tutor in San Jose State University. Then, I have worked as IBM Db2 tools tester for a year while pursuing my B.S. degree in Computer Science, and currently as DFSMS development team lead in IBM.\n\nBenefiting from the different projects I have worked with several STSMs, I have worked really well with people across IBM infrastructure such as DFSMS and BCP to achieve the solution design for each project. I have worked in harmony with my team members so that we can achieve the common goal which is to deliver as many supported and own projects for DFSMS as we can since I was assigned as a team lead. \n\nI consistently attempt to be candid, open, and transparent with my team members about the requirements our squad will need to observe and execute on related to release level and service related work items. I take an active role in helping my team members feel empowered and accountable towards the ownership and design of their assigned projects. I am also a strong resource for my team where I provide advisement and assistance on the direction of their design. I am committed to this trusted growth behavior because I want my team member to grow their career skills and I too can grow my team leadership skills as well.\n\nAs a team lead, I handle all the customer requests for enhancement. I have listened carefully to all the customers for their request to understand what they are looking for. Then, I will try to see how our current code behaves before accepting their request or communicating back for more information. In the z/OS release 2.2, when I received the request that the customer wanted to monitor their storage group space usage, I discussed with my team members about what I understood about the request. All my team members who were retired from IBM thought we just needed to enhance an existing message. I went back to the customers and ask for more information. Then, I proposed to my team that I would like to develop a project called \"Storage Group Alert Messages\" which would be actively monitoring the storage group space usage. As result, when my project was delivered to the Beta customers, my project got voted as number one for customer satisfaction.",
